gaps.proposal:
Move the tools into scripts/anatomy_validation/ (library side, run on demand) and add a vitest suite, src/render/xray/anatomy.test.ts, that generates 50 genomes and checks projected metrics against ranges measured from the library:
- cranial convention;
- proximal LAD/LCx height ≤ bifurcation + 3 mm;
- 8-20 mm tortuosity band RMS within 6-22°;
- RCA/LAD motion amplitude ratio 1.6-3;
- twist sign (apex counter-clockwise);
- non-zero root motion;
- still fraction falling with HR;
- bifurcation exponent between Murray and Huo-Kassab bounds;
- vessel-to-silhouette distance ≤ 3 mm at the margins;
- extent and transect-count ranges per view.

Create src/simulation/patient.ts: a single 'patient engine' that runs circulation.step() at its DT in a worker and owns heart rate, rhythm, blood pressure, LV volume, coronary flow per territory and pericardial volume. Cath-lab actions publish events to it:
- balloon or stent inflation occludes Q_cor in that territory, causing regional ischaemia and ST elevation in the leads facing it, chest pain prompts, and hypotension for proximal large territories;
- guide deep seating produces damping or ventricularisation from the catheter rod's wall contact;
- contrast load and injection time add transient bradycardia;
- slow flow or no-reflow reduces flow and BP;
- perforation adds an extravasation rate into the pericardium, raising pericardial pressure, then tamponade physiology, which hands off to pericardiocentesis.ts;
- drugs (heparin with an ACT clock, intracoronary nitrate, adenosine for FFR, atropine, vasopressor) have pharmacodynamic time constants.
Show VitalsMonitor.tsx (ECG and pressure waveform) beside the PCI image and record every response in the event log for the debrief. The coronary flow used by the contrast transport in the previous gap comes from the same engine, so contrast physics and haemodynamics are consistent.

(1) Add a parametric patient generator in caseGenerator.ts:
- habitus 0.85-1.25, which scales thorax.ts body and soft tissue and therefore AEC kV, noise and dose;
- heart scale and axis (horizontal or vertical heart, cardiomegaly, LV hypertrophy) through makeHeart(scale, axisAngles);
- diaphragm height and lung volume;
- aortic unfolding and root tilt, already in anatomy.ts.
(2) Real coronary geometry: with the user's approval for the download and after checking the licence, write scripts/cta_trees/build_trees.py, which skeletonises lumen labels into centreline plus radius, fits the heart ellipsoid frame to the myocardium, and stores each vessel in (ell, phi, h) surface coordinates as JSON. The existing per-frame motion then applies unchanged. Load a random real tree per case.
(3) Sample lesion locations and grades from the AngioCAD joint distribution conditioned on indication, and generate the vignette from the same sample.
This gives effectively endless distinct patients whose anatomy is real rather than templated.

Add src/render/xray/hardware.ts, returning TubeSpec and BonePrim lists with per-case random seeds:
- ECG electrodes as small dense discs with a metal snap, on the chest wall surface, moving with breathing;
- lead wires as sagging catenary tubes running off the field;
- in post-CABG cases (a new case variant), sternal wires as twisted loops along the sternum and surgical clips along the graft course;
- pacemaker leads running from a subclavian can into the RV, moving with the heart;
- valve rings and TAVI frames from the structural modules;
- the table edge and mattress as a box with a slight gradient.
Metal goes in the B channel. Make it switchable for teaching ('remove clutter').

Model each complication as a change to geometry and flow, so that it shows up in the image and the physiology through the existing pipeline:
- dissection: a second lumen tube with a separating flap and its own advection compartment that retains contrast (staining) and can extend with forceful injections or wire passes; downstream TIMI flow drops;
- perforation: an Ellis-type jet whose extravasation rate feeds pericardial volume in the patient engine;
- stent over a side branch: carina shift that narrows the side-branch ostium by an amount driven by plaque, bifurcation angle and the stent-to-main-vessel ratio, using published risk factors as qualitative drivers; then wire re-crossing, kissing balloons and proximal optimisation as actions in angioPci.ts;
- air: lucent bubbles advected in the iodine column, with ST elevation and bradycardia in the patient engine;
- thrombus: fragments embolising to distal twigs (occludedFrom on twig vessels), giving slow flow.

